  FL-001: The Palace is a transactional database in the cloud on GCP that agents on many machines reach, behaving the same for every agent and sub-agent wherever it runs.
  FL-002: Memory units have a length cap; a memory over the cap is shortened to fit as one memory, and it is split only when it holds more than one distinct fact, with the split logged.
  FL-006: Every memory carries a location column and a project column.
  FL-007: Every memory links to the full conversation it came from, so an agent can reach more than just the memory when needed.
  FL-009: Record files (history, transcripts) live apart from code so code can be deleted and re-downloaded without losing history.
  FL-010: Every thread has a thread id, and every memory records the thread it was born in.
  FL-044: At compaction the summarizing agent turns anything important but not needed for the next few build steps into a memory through the normal memory creation path, and compaction waits for those memories to land before dropping the rest.
  FL-066: A repeatable build benchmark: build the same thing in rounds against one accumulating palace and measure time, lines and cost falling.
  FL-068: Injected memories refreshed on every move.
  FL-069: The core tools every modern harness has: files, terminal, Chrome, skills, changing models, compaction (our own take), and interactions with common apps, taken from the most modular standard source.
  FL-072: The progressive prompting protocol is dynamic to the agent's moves: a move re-prompts the agent for its new location.
  FL-073: The location rule written into Nocturne's own system prompt.
  FL-075: Queuing prompts and interjecting or steering a running agent, like the modern harnesses do.
  FL-085: The work loop: spec alignment with the user, then the agent loop (optionally several agents in worktrees attempting the same thing), then the judge checks the work against the spec and either calls it done or sends another agent to continue or fix, and the human is only brought back in when the judge says done.
  FL-197: Spend and Palace state reads use the calling principal; only the configured owner can request the whole Palace.
  FL-162: Easy onboarding from a single splash repo, needing only two secrets: a GCP credential and an OpenRouter key.
  FL-164: Local mode with no internet, cloud mode, and a way to mirror our cloud setup into your own provider.
  FL-166: A prompt when the app's memory use passes a threshold of the local system's memory, memory-leak guards, and no proceeding without write access at startup.
  FL-167: One obvious start command with an update prompt; update, deploy, launch and doctor as one or two commands.
  FL-168: A restore popup that asks whether to roll back these memories and lists them.
  FL-170: A fresh reinstall that reconnects to existing files and auto-finds the cloud palace.
  FL-186: Build on Pydantic AI in a way that lets us modify and extend without waiting on them, passing features back and forth with their library.
  FL-190: The Harness can use skills and has the best and most common tools available, compiled from a list.
  FL-192: Swappable seams: router, model policy with an off-switch, toolset as a unit, memory system.
  FL-193: The common core of terminal-agent tools (skills, progressive prompting, model switching, compaction, app interactions) in one piece that pulls upstream updates.
